  "content": {
    "id": "p15-q16-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Given the points $A(-2,2)$ and $B(0,4)$, find the point $P$ on the graph of $y=3x$ for which $AP=BP$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$P\\left(\\frac12,\\frac32\\right)$.\n\nWrite $P=(x,3x)$ and equate the squared distances from $A$ and $B$.",
      "worked": "Since $P$ lies on $y=3x$, write $P=(x,3x)$. Using $AP=BP$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\nAP^2&=BP^2\\\\\n(x+2)^2+(3x-2)^2&=x^2+(3x-4)^2\\\\\n10x^2-8x+8&=10x^2-24x+16\\\\\n16x&=8\\\\\nx&=\\frac12.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThen\n$$\\begin{align*}\ny&=3\\left(\\frac12\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac32.\n\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $P\\left(\\frac12,\\frac32\\right)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 88
  },
